    {# May 2026 #}
    <article class="build-update reveal">
      <header class="update-header">
        <h3>May 2026</h3>
        <span class="update-status status-active">Current</span>
      </header>
      
      <div class="update-content">
        <h4>What I Did</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Published 3 new articles — Plumber Marketing, Plumber Marketing Agency, HVAC Marketing Strategy</li>
          <li>Started local citation building (in progress — not finished yet)</li>
          <li>Refined sales process and prospect research workflow</li>
          <li>Built out content roadmap targeting low-difficulty, high-volume keywords</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h4>What's Working</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Articles continue to get indexed by Google quickly</li>
          <li>The articles I'm writing target keywords with real search volume and beatable competition — "contractor website design" scores 22/100 difficulty with 1,000 monthly searches</li>
          <li>SERP analysis shows my niche (contractor-specific) isn't being targeted by the generic web design agencies ranking in my area</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h4>What's Not</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Still zero organic traffic. The site is still effectively invisible to Google.</li>
          <li>Article cadence was lumpy this month — three articles on the same day instead of spaced out. Steady is better than batchy.</li>
          <li>Local citations are started but not finished. This is the unsexy work that builds authority and it's been slow.</li>
          <li>Founding Client Program: still 3 spots open. No one's signed yet.</li>
          <li>Still zero external backlinks. Same as last month.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h4>The Honest Read</h4>
        <p>Two and a half months in. Fourteen articles written. Still nothing to show in traffic or rankings. This is what month two looks like for almost everyone doing content marketing the right way — you do the work and the work doesn't pay you back yet. The temptation is to call it a failed strategy and chase ads. The discipline is to keep stacking shovels of dirt. So that's what I'm doing.</p>
        
        <h4>Next Month's Focus</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Finish local citation build — Yelp, BBB, Nextdoor, Manta, Chamber of Commerce</li>
          <li>Move to a steady weekly publishing cadence — one Wednesday, one Saturday — instead of batch drops</li>
          <li>Real outreach for backlinks: guest posts, podcast interviews, anywhere a contractor-marketing voice fits</li>
          <li>Land the first Founding Client — even one signed deal validates the model</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

